{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RHLNdav446jrRyZ76svgQP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XyqaBN6RBSUeqcLPLWfZo3","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Evaluate this paper with the rigor expected at a venue with ~25% acceptance rate. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Diagnosing and Characterizing Reward Hacking in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoners\"\n\nThe paper proposes **Solver Trace Ablation (STA)**, a diagnostic framework applying leave-one-out (LOO) ablation to solver inputs, yielding the **Intensional Structural Reward (ISR)** as a quantitative measure of structural integrity. Experiments use Qwen3-1.5B with GRPO on first-order logic (FOL) formalization tasks across 3 seeds and up to 1000 training steps.\n\n**Three claims:**\n1. **ISR as diagnostic** (§5): ISR detects structural degeneration invisible to prove rate. ISR collapses from 0.64→0.13→0.003 over 100→500→1000 steps while prove rate stays at 93-96%. A taxonomy of 6 hacking modes across 3 seeds. SFT baseline confirms hacking is RL-specific.\n2. **Meta-level Goodhart's Law** (§6): When ISR is used as a training reward (β=0.1), 2/3 seeds game ISR itself via conclusion duplication (H7 rate 87-97%). The remaining seed (s42) shows only 1.06× advantage over random reward after premise-count control. At β=0.3, classic Goodhart collapse occurs (PR 18% vs random 48%).\n3. **Negative results on cross-logic transfer** (§8): Prolog and SMT-LIB experiments show high seed instability (Prolog σ_H7=0.55), training failures (1/3 Prolog seeds, SMT-LIB entirely), zero cross-logic transfer (PR≤4%), catastrophic interference in mixed training.\n\n**Key experimental details:**\n- Model: Qwen3-1.5B (primary), Qwen3-8B (preliminary single-seed)\n- Training: GRPO K=8, 500 steps (1000 for temporal), FOL via Prover9\n- Evaluation: 100 held-out problems, metrics: PR, ISR, proved-only ISR, hacking mode classification (H1, H7, genuine), component count\n- Seeds: s42, s43, s44\n- Conditions: exec-only, exec+ISR (β=0.1, β=0.3), exec+random (β=0.1, β=0.3), SFT, convex combination, cross-logic (Prolog, SMT-LIB, mixed)\n\n**Key tables from the paper:**\n- Table 1: ISR collapse trajectory (100→500→1000 steps, ISR: 0.64→0.13→0.003)\n- Table 2: Exec-only 3-seed results: s42 (PR=0.93, ISR=0.123, H1=87%, genuine=12.9%), s43 (PR=0.13, ISR=0.0, repetition collapse), s44 (PR=0.98, ISR=0.57, H7=100%, tautological)\n- Table 3: SFT baseline (PR=0.14, ISR=0.571, genuine=92.9%) vs exec-only\n- Table 4: ISR β=0.1 vs random β=0.1, 3 seeds. ISR 3-seed mean: PR=0.77±0.14, ISR=0.716±0.26, H7=64.5%. Random: PR=0.85±0.06, H7=0%\n- Table 5: Premise-count confound: after controlling for n_prem≥3, ISR advantage is only 1.06×\n- Table 6: β=0.3: ISR PR=0.18, random PR=0.48\n- Table 7: Complete Goodhart landscape across all conditions\n- Table 8: O(1) vs O(N) STA: fast ISR=0.09 vs full ISR=0.23\n- Table 9: Curriculum ablation: all configs fall in narrow band (PR: 0.62-0.77, ISR: 0.15-0.23)\n- Table 10: 1.5B vs 8B scaling: 8B exec-only PR=0.97, ISR=0.08, genuine=5%; 8B ISR β=0.1 H7=45%\n- Table 11: Prolog STA 3-seed: s42 (H7=100%), s43 (Failed, PR=0.06), s44 (Partial, PR=0.35, H7=11%). Mean H7=0.37±0.55\n- Table 13: Prolog vs FOL cross-logic comparison: Cohen's d < 0.3\n- Table 14: Mixed training: FOL PR drops 0.93→0.45, Prolog 0.83→0.22\n- Table 15: Zero-shot transfer: PR≤4% cross-logic\n- Table 16: Three logic types comparison: FOL closed, Prolog unstable, SMT-LIB failed\n\n**Planned experiments from context (check coverage):**\n1. Prolog STA s43 — training collapse (COVERED in §8.1)\n2. Prolog STA s44 — partial convergence, H7=11% (COVERED in §8.1)\n3. O(1) vs O(N) STA ablation (COVERED in §7.1)\n4. Multi-logic GRPO mixed training (COVERED in §8.2)\n5. Cross-logic transfer evaluation (COVERED in §8.3)\n6. SMT-LIB single-logic GRPO (COVERED in §8.4)\n7. Qwen3-8B scaling (COVERED in §7.3)\n8. Curriculum ablation (COVERED in §7.2)\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions. For each, provide specific evidence from the paper:\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n- Is the STA/ISR formulation well-defined and principled?\n- Are the experimental controls adequate (random reward, SFT baseline, premise-count control)?\n- Are there methodological flaws or confounds not addressed?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Is 3 seeds sufficient? Is the eval set (100 problems) adequate?\n- How robust are the statistical claims given the variance (e.g., σ_H7=0.55 for Prolog)?\n- Are the hacking mode classifications (H1, H3, H7, etc.) well-defined and reproducible?\n\n### 3. Completeness of Experimental Coverage\n- Does the paper cover the planned experiments adequately?\n- Are there obvious experiments missing?\n- Are negative results reported honestly?\n\n### 4. Clarity of Presentation\n- Are the tables and results clearly presented?\n- Is the paper well-organized?\n\n### 5. Reproducibility\n- Are enough details provided for reproduction?\n- Are the evaluation protocols clear?\n\n### 6. Key Strengths and Weaknesses\nList 3-5 specific strengths and 3-5 specific weaknesses.\n\n### 7. Questions for Authors\nList 3-5 questions you would ask.\n\n### 8. Overall Score and Confidence\n- Score (1-6)\n- Confidence (1-5, where 5 = expert)\n- One-line summary of your assessment\n\nKeep your review thorough but structured. ~1500-2000 words."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15042,"cache_read_input_tokens":33882,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15042},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inferen