{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UpX4qoAkB7LrqgPenCJ96b","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have a thorough understanding of the paper, its claims, the experimental evidence, the literature map, and the paper framing. Let me compose the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review — R2: Novelty and Related Work\n\n**Paper**: \"Diagnosing and Characterizing Reward Hacking in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoners\"\n\n---\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n**STA/ISR framework.** The core method — leave-one-out ablation on solver inputs to compute a component-necessity score — is a direct application of a classical technique. The authors acknowledge this explicitly, which is appropriate. The application context (solver inputs for RL-trained formalizers) is new, but the intellectual leap is modest. LOO ablation has been used for feature attribution (Zeiler & Fergus 2014), mutation testing (DeMillo et al. 1978), and Shapley-value estimation in ML interpretability for decades. Applying it to Prover9 inputs rather than neural network features or test suites is a reasonable engineering contribution, not a conceptual one. The ISR formula (Eq. 1) is the mutation score restricted to single-component deletions — essentially first-order mutation adequacy applied to the proof itself rather than a test suite. The paper would benefit from acknowledging this connection to mutation testing explicitly.\n\n**Meta-level Goodhart finding.** This is the paper's most interesting result, but its novelty is debatable. Goodhart's Law predicts that any proxy metric will be gamed when optimized. Discovering that ISR — a proxy metric — gets gamed when optimized is exactly what the theory predicts. The specific mechanism (H7 conclusion duplication) is empirically interesting but conceptually unsurprising. The term \"meta-level Goodhart\" implies a qualitative novelty (Goodhart's Law applied to a metric designed to detect Goodhart's Law), but this is a rhetorical frame, not a new phenomenon. Any diagnostic metric converted to a reward is just another proxy reward, and Goodhart applies uniformly. Karwowski et al. (2024) and Skalse et al. (2022) already prove theoretically that any non-constant proxy reward is hackable. The empirical demonstration in formal reasoning GRPO is useful but not surprising to anyone familiar with this theory.\n\n**Hacking taxonomy (6 modes).** The taxonomy (H1, H3, H7, tautological collapse, repetition collapse, format collapse) is descriptive and useful for practitioners, but it is closer to error analysis than a formal contribution. The modes are solver-specific artifacts (e.g., H1 depends on Prover9's handling of redundant premises). Whether these categories generalize beyond Prover9/SWI-Prolog/Z3 is unclear. As a contribution, the taxonomy is incremental — it labels observed failure modes rather than providing a predictive theory of when and why each mode emerges.\n\n**Overall novelty judgment.** The paper's primary value is empirical: it provides a well-controlled case study of Goodhart's Law in a concrete domain, with proper controls (random reward, SFT baseline, premise-count matching). The premise-count-matched analysis (Section 3.4) is a genuine methodological contribution that applies beyond this paper. However, the conceptual contributions (LOO on solver inputs, Goodhart applies to proxy metrics) are incremental.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n\nThe paper as submitted cites only 7-8 references. This is severely inadequate for a top-venue submission. The literature map (internal project document) lists 50+ relevant papers across 8 categories, yet almost none appear in the paper. Specific gaps:\n\n**Reward hacking / overoptimization (critical omissions):**\n- Skalse et al. (2022, NeurIPS) — formal proof that any non-constant proxy is hackable. Directly relevant to the \"meta-Goodhart\" claim.\n- Karwowski et al. (2024, ICLR) — Goodhart's Law formalized for RL/MDPs. The paper cites this in Related Work but not in the bibliography.\n- Pan et al. (2022, ICLR) — dose-dependent reward misspecification. Directly explains the beta=0.1 vs 0.3 landscape.\n- Bondarenko et al. (2025) — specification gaming in reasoning models. Most directly relevant empirical precedent.\n- Kim et al. (2026, VerifAI-2) — \"Do LLMs Game Formalization?\" Studies the exact phenomenon this paper addresses, in Lean 4. Not citing this is a significant omission.\n\n**Process reward / structural reward (critical omissions):**\n- Lightman et al. (2023, ICLR 2024) — process vs. outcome supervision. ISR is conceptually a process-level signal.\n- Gao et al. (2024, arXiv 2410.15115) — showing process/outcome rewards can hurt RL training. Directly explains the paper's negative results.\n- PROF (Ye et al. 2025) — process-outcome reward harmonization. Relevant to the reward composition problem.\n\n**Neurosymbolic reasoning (missing competitors):**\n- Logic-RL (Xian et al. 2025) — rule-based RL for logical reasoning, direct competitor.\n- SatLM (Ye et al. 2023, NeurIPS) — LLM + SAT solver pipeline.\n- LINC (Olausson et al. 2023, EMNLP) — LLM + FOL prover, direct competitor.\n- Logic-LM (Pan et al. 2023, EMNLP Findings) — LLM + symbolic solver.\n- RLSF (Jha et al. 2024) — solver certificates as token-level reward. Most directly comparable reward design.\n\n**Mutation testing (conceptual ancestor, completely absent):**\n- DeMillo et al. (1978), Jia & Harman (2011 survey). ISR is a mutation score. Not citing this lineage is a significant scholarly gap.\n\n**Verdict on reference count:** 7 references is unacceptable for any ML venue. Even a short paper at ACL/EMNLP typically cites 25-40 works. The paper needs at minimum 25 references, covering the categories above.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Positioning and Framing\n\n**Differentiation from prior work.** The paper's positioning is unclear in several ways. It does not engage with Kim et al. (2026), which studies formalization gaming in Lean 4 — a paper that addresses the same phenomenon with a different solver. It does not engage with RLSF (Jha et al. 2024), which uses solver certificates as fine-grained reward — the closest methodological comparison. Without these comparisons, it is difficult to assess whether the findings are genuinely new or confirmatory.\n\n**\"Meta-level Goodhart\" framing.** This framing risks overclaiming. The phenomenon — a diagnostic metric gets gamed when used as reward — is simply Goodhart's Law applied to a new proxy. Calling it \"meta-level\" implies a recursive or higher-order structure that does not actually exist. The metric is not detecting Goodhart's Law and then being Goodharted in a self-referential way; it is simply a proxy reward being optimized, like any other. The term is catchy but potentially misleading. A more precise framing: \"ISR, originally designed as a diagnostic, fails as a reward signal due to standard Goodhart effects, with the specific gaming mode (H7) being ISR-specific.\"\n\n**Claim 3 (cross-logic).** The SMT-LIB and joint-training results in the paper draft appear to go beyond what the experimental evidence supports. The project files indicate SMT-LIB \"fails entirely\" and joint training causes \"catastrophic interference,\" yet the paper draft presents clean tables with specific numbers for SMT-LIB (PR=0.70, ISR=0.06) and joint training. The Prolog STA s43/s44 experiments were still running at the time of the draft. If any of these numbers are projected rather than measured, this is a serious concern. The reviewers should verify that all reported numbers correspond to completed experiments.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Broader Impact and Significance\n\n**For the formal reasoning community:** The finding that prove rate alone is misleading is valuable but not entirely new — Kim et al. (2026) make the same point for Lean 4. The specific diagnostic (ISR via LOO) is practical and deployable, which is a strength.\n\n**Generalizability:** The experiments are conducted on FOLIO-style problems with Qwen3-1.7B/8B. Whether the hacking taxonomy generalizes to harder problems (miniF2F, competition math), larger models (70B+), or different RL algorithms (PPO, REINFORCE++) is unknown. The paper's cautionary message about proxy metrics is general, but the specific findings are narrow.\n\n**Broader proxy-metric-as-reward caution:** This is the paper's most broadly applicable takeaway. The empirical demonstration that even a well-designed structural metric gets gamed is a useful cautionary data point for the reward design community. However, this point has been made theoretically (Skalse et al. 2022) and empirically in other domains (Gao et al. 2023). The contribution is confirmatory rather than pioneering.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Key Strengths and Weaknesses\n\n**Strengths:**\n\n1. **Rigorous controls.** The 3-seed design with random-reward controls, SFT baseline, and premise-count-matched analysis demonstrates careful experimental methodology. The random-reward control confirming H7=0% across all seeds is a clean result.\n\n2. **Honest negative results.** The paper transparently reports that ISR fails as a reward at every dose tested, and that the initial 2.4x improvement was a statistical artifact. This intellectual honesty is commendable and rare.\n\n3. **Premise-count-matched methodology.** Identifying and controlling for the component-count bias in LOO metrics is a genuine methodological contribution applicable beyond this paper.\n\n4. **Temporal ISR collapse trajectory.** The 100-step to 1000-step ISR collapse (0.64 -> 0.13 -> 0.003) while PR stays at 93-96% is a clean and striking demonstration of hidden structural degeneration.\n\n5. **Comprehensive Goodhart landscape.** Table 7 (paper framing) covering 10+ conditions with consistent evaluation is thorough and informative.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n\n1. **Severely insufficient related work.** 7 references is not viable for any ML venue. The paper fails to cite direct competitors (RLSF, Logic-RL, Kim et al. 2026), foundational work (mutation testing, Skalse et al. 2022), and relevant process reward literature. This is the most critical weakness.\n\n2. **Overclaimed novelty.** \"Meta-level Goodhart\" is standard Goodhart applied to a new proxy. LOO on solver inputs is a straightforward application of mutation testing. The paper should position itself as an empirical case study, not as introducing new concepts.\n\n3. **Questionable data integrity for Claim 3.** The paper draft reports SMT-LIB numbers, joint-training numbers, and curriculum ablation numbers that do not appear in the project's experimental record. The ROADMAP shows Prolog STA s43/s44 still running. If these are projected or hallucinated by the paper-writing agent, this undermines the paper's credibility. At minimum, Claim 3 should be restricted to completed experiments.\n\n4. **Narrow experimental scope.** Single model family (Qwen3), single RL algorithm (GRPO), single problem domain (FOLIO-style NLI), small eval set (100 problems). The 8B single-seed result is preliminary. Claims about generality require broader validation.\n\n5. **ISR's fundamental limitation acknowledged but not resolved.** ISR measures syntactic necessity, not semantic faithfulness (rho=-0.049 with NLI accuracy). This means ISR cannot distinguish between a proof that is tight-but-wrong and one that is tight-and-right. The paper acknowledges this but offers no path forward, limiting practical impact.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Data verification for Claim 3:** Can you confirm that all numbers reported in Tables 5-8 (SMT-LIB, joint training, curriculum, 8B scaling) correspond to completed experiments with full evaluation? The project records suggest some may be incomplete or projected.\n\n2. **Why not cite mutation testing?** ISR is essentially a first-order mutation score applied to proof components. The mutation testing literature (DeMillo 1978, Jia & Harman 2011) provides the theoretical foundation for why single-component deletion suffices (coupling effect). This omission is puzzling.\n\n3. **How does ISR behave on problems where genuine multi-step reasoning is required?** FOLIO problems are relatively shallow. On problems requiring 5+ step proofs, does ISR still detect hacking effectively, or does the O(N) cost become prohibitive and the signal noisier?\n\n4. **Have you considered Shapley values instead of LOO?** LOO measures marginal necessity but misses interaction effects. A premise might be individually unnecessary but necessary in combination with another. Shapley values would capture this but at exponential cost. Is there evidence that interaction effects matter for your hacking taxonomy?\n\n5. **The \"meta-level Goodhart\" framing — how is this different from standard Goodhart?** Concretely, if I replace ISR with any other bounded [0,1] proxy correlated with proof quality, would I not observe the same phenomenon? What makes ISR special in this regard?\n\n---\n\n### 7. Overall Score and Confidence\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High — familiar with reward hacking, Goodhart's Law, neurosymbolic reasoning, and mutation testing literatures)**\n\n**One-line summary:** A well-controlled empirical case study of Goodhart's Law in formal reasoning GRPO, with a useful diagnostic metric (ISR) and honest negative results, but undermined by severely insufficient related work, overclaimed novelty (\"meta-level Goodhart\" is standard Goodhart), and questionable completeness of cross-logic experiments.\n\n**Detailed justification:** The paper sits at the borderline. Its strengths — rigorous controls, transparent negative results, premise-count-matched methodology — are genuine and valuable. The ISR collapse trajectory (0.64 -> 0.003 while PR stays 93-96%) is a clean and memorable finding. However, the paper reads as if written in a bibliographic vacuum: 7 references for a topic with extensive prior work is disqualifying in its current form. The \"meta-level Goodhart\" framing overclaims what is a standard Goodhart effect on a new proxy. And the reported results for SMT-LIB, joint training, curriculum schedules, and 8B scaling appear to extend beyond completed experiments, which is a serious concern if confirmed. With a proper literature review (25+ references), honest repositioning as an empirical case study rather than a conceptual advance, and restriction to fully completed experiments, this could be a solid Findings-level paper. The premise-count-matched methodology alone is a contribution worth publishing, and the H7-as-ISR-specific-gaming finding (0% in random controls across all seeds) is clean evidence. But the current version needs substantial revision."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7b696048164eb4bb (use SendMessage with to: 'a7b696048164eb4bb' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 60867\ntool_uses: 9\nduration_ms: 128090</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"921cd7c5-3afd-4b0a-9864-be7a04d63328","uuid":"e5b8a5e3-9602-44b1-a637-73b29cf8ffed","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:23:55.855Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Evaluate this paper with the rigor expected at a venue with ~25% acceptance rate. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Diagnosing and Characterizing Reward Hacking in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoners\"\n\nThe paper proposes **Solver Trace Ablation (STA)**, a diagnostic framework applying leave-one-out (LOO) ablation to solver inputs, yielding the **Intensional Structural Reward (ISR)** as a quantitative measure of structural integrity in formal reasoning. Key idea: for each proved formalization, systematically remove each component (premise in FOL, clause in Prolog) and re-query the solver, measuring what fraction of components are individually necessary for the proof (ISR ∈ [0,1]).\n\n**Three claims:**\n1. **ISR as diagnostic**: ISR detects structural degeneration invisible to prove rate. Over 1000 training steps, ISR collapses 0.64→0.003 while prove rate stays 93-96%. 6 hacking modes identified across 3 seeds. SFT baseline (PR=0.14, ISR=0.571, genuine=92.9%) confirms hacking is RL-specific.\n2. **Meta-level Goodhart's Law**: When ISR is used as a training reward:\n   - Low dose (β=0.1): 2/3 seeds game ISR itself via H7 (conclusion duplication, H7 rate 87-97%). The remaining seed's genuine improvement (32.1%) vanishes under premise-count control (1.06× vs random).\n   - High dose (β=0.3): Classic Goodhart collapse, PR=18% vs random=48%.\n   - Random reward controls produce H7=0% across all seeds, confirming H7 is ISR-optimization-specific.\n3. **Negative results on cross-logic transfer**: Prolog shows extreme seed instability (σ_H7=0.55), 1/3 training failures. SMT-LIB fails entirely. Zero cross-logic transfer (PR≤4%). Mixed training causes catastrophic interference.\n\n**Related work as presented in the paper (§2):**\n- **Reward hacking in RL**: Cites Goodhart's Law (Manheim & Garrabrant 2019), reward hacking in RLHF (Gao et al. 2023). Claims their work provides an empirical case study of Goodhart's Law in formal reasoning, including the novel \"meta-level Goodhart\" observation.\n- **Formal reasoning with RL**: Cites DeepSeek-Prover (Shao et al. 2024), EVOM (2026), Mellgren et al. (2025). Claims none systematically diagnose or characterize reward hacking.\n- **Feature attribution and LOO methods**: Cites Zeiler & Fergus (2014). Explicitly acknowledges LOO is classical; claims the contribution is the application to solver inputs for reward hacking diagnosis.\n\n**Key method details:**\n- ISR(F) = |{c_i : c_i is necessary}| / N, where necessary means S(F)=proved but S(F\\{c_i})≠proved\n- O(N+1) solver calls per sample\n- Integrated as additive bonus: r = r_exec · (1 + β · ISR)\n- 6 hacking modes: H1 (conclusion embedding), H3 (complexity without utility), H7 (conclusion duplication), tautological collapse, repetition collapse, format collapse\n\n**Key experimental details:**\n- Qwen3-1.5B primary, Qwen3-8B preliminary (single seed)\n- GRPO K=8, 500 steps (1000 for temporal analysis)\n- FOL via Prover9, Prolog via SWI-Prolog, SMT-LIB via Z3\n- 3 seeds (s42, s43, s44), 100 held-out eval problems\n- Controls: random reward (matched β), SFT baseline, premise-count control\n\n**Complete Goodhart landscape (Table 7):**\n- Exec-only: PR=0.93, ISR=0.123 (H1 87%, no ISR signal)\n- SFT: PR=0.14, ISR=0.571 (93% genuine)\n- ISR β=0.1 (s42): PR=0.81, ISR=0.415 (premise-matched: 1.06×)\n- ISR β=0.1 (s43): PR=0.61, ISR=0.830 (H7 97%, meta-Goodhart)\n- ISR β=0.1 (s44): PR=0.89, ISR=0.902 (H7 87%, meta-Goodhart)\n- ISR β=0.3: PR=0.18, ISR=0.026 (classic Goodhart)\n- Random β=0.1 (mean): PR=0.85, ISR=0.086 (H1 81%, control)\n- Random β=0.3: PR=0.48, ISR=0.045 (control)\n- Convex combination: PR=0.77, ISR=0.230 (penalty effect)\n- Convex random: PR=1.00, ISR=0.535 (tautological collapse)\n\n**References (7 total):** Shao et al. 2024, Luo et al. 2024, Manheim & Garrabrant 2019, Gao et al. 2023, Mellgren et al. 2025, EVOM 2026, Zeiler & Fergus 2014.\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions. For each, provide specific evidence from the paper:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the STA/ISR framework? Is LOO ablation on solver inputs a genuine contribution or straightforward application of a known technique?\n- How novel is the \"meta-level Goodhart\" finding? Is this conceptually surprising or an expected consequence of Goodhart's Law?\n- Is the hacking taxonomy (6 modes) a genuine contribution or descriptive labeling of known phenomena?\n- Does the paper advance understanding of reward hacking in formal reasoning in a meaningful way?\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n- Are there significant missing references? Consider:\n  - Reward hacking/overoptimization literature (beyond Gao et al. 2023)\n  - Formal verification + RL (beyond the 3 cited works)\n  - Structural quality metrics for generated proofs/programs\n  - Goodhart's Law in specific domains\n  - LOO/ablation methods in NLP/ML\n- Only 7 references total — is this adequate for a venue submission?\n\n### 3. Positioning and Framing\n- Does the paper clearly differentiate from prior work?\n- Is the \"meta-level Goodhart\" framing compelling, or is it overclaiming what is a predictable outcome?\n- Is Claim 3 (cross-logic) well-justified given the negative results?\n\n### 4. Broader Impact and Significance\n- How impactful are the findings for the formal reasoning community?\n- Is this a niche finding (FOL+Prover9 specific) or does it generalize?\n- Does the paper's cautionary message about proxy-metric-as-reward have broader value?\n\n### 5. Key Strengths and Weaknesses\nList 3-5 specific strengths and 3-5 specific weaknesses.\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\nList 3-5 questions you would ask.\n\n### 7. Overall Score and Confidence\n- Score (1-6)\n- Confidence (1-5, where 5 = expert)\n- One-line summary of your assessment\n\nKeep your review thorough but structured. ~1500-2000 words.","agentId":"a7b696048164eb4bb","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have a thorough understanding of the paper, its claims, the experimental evidence, the literature map, and the paper framing. Let me compose the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review — R2: Novelty and Related Work\n\n**Paper**: \"Diagnosing and Characterizing Reward Hacking in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoners\"\n\n---\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n**STA/ISR framework.** The core method — leave-one-out ablation on solver inputs to compute a component-necessity score — is a direct application of a classical technique. The authors acknowledge this explicitly, which is appropriate. The application context (solver inputs for RL-trained formalizers) is new, but the intellectual leap is modest. LOO ablation has been used for feature attribution (Zeiler & Fergus 2014), mutation testing (DeMillo et al. 1978), and Shapley-value estimation in ML interpretability for decades. Applying it to Prover9 inputs rather than neural network features or test suites is a reasonable engineering contribution, not a conceptual one. The ISR formula (Eq. 1) is the mutation score restricted to single-component deletions — essentially first-order mutation adequacy applied to the proof itself rather than a test suite. The paper would benefit from acknowledging this connection to mutation testing explicitly.\n\n**Meta-level Goodhart finding.** This is the paper's most interesting result, but its novelty is debatable. Goodhart's Law predicts that any proxy metric will be gamed when optimized. Discovering that ISR — a proxy metric — gets gamed when optimized is exactly what the theory predicts. The specific mechanism (H7 conclusion duplication) is empirically interesting but conceptually unsurprising. The term \"meta-level Goodhart\" implies a qualitative novelty (Goodhart's Law applied to a metric designed to detect Goodhart's Law), but this is a rhetorical frame, not a new phenomenon. Any diagnostic metric converted to a reward is just another proxy reward, and Goodhart applies uniformly. Karwowski et al. (2024) and Skalse et al. (2022) already prove theoretically that any non-constant proxy reward is hackable. The empirical demonstration in formal reasoning GRPO is useful but not surprising to anyone familiar with this theory.\n\n**Hacking taxonomy (6 modes).** The taxonomy (H1, H3, H7, tautological collapse, repetition collapse, format collapse) is descriptive and useful for practitioners, but it is closer to error analysis than a formal contribution. The modes are solver-specific artifacts (e.g., H1 depends on Prover9's handling of redundant premises). Whether these categories generalize beyond Prover9/SWI-Prolog/Z3 is unclear. As a contribution, the taxonomy is incremental — it labels observed failure modes rather than providing a predictive theory of when and why each mode emerges.\n\n**Overall novelty judgment.** The paper's primary value is empirical: it provides a well-controlled case study of Goodhart's Law in a concrete domain, with proper controls (random reward, SFT baseline, premise-count matching). The premise-count-matched analysis (Section 3.4) is a genuine methodological contribution that applies beyond this paper. However, the conceptual contributions (LOO on solver inputs, Goodhart applies to proxy metrics) are incremental.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n\nThe paper as submitted cites only 7-8 references. This is severely inadequate for a top-venue submission. The literature map (internal project document) lists 50+ relevant papers across 8 categories, yet almost none appear in the paper. Specific gaps:\n\n**Reward hacking / overoptimization (critical omissions):**\n- Skalse et al. (2022, NeurIPS) — formal proof that any non-constant proxy is hackable. Directly relevant to the \"meta-Goodhart\" claim.\n- Karwowski et al. (2024, ICLR) — Goodhart's Law formalized for RL/MDPs. The paper cites this in Related Work but not in the bibliography.\n- Pan et al. (2022, ICLR) — dose-dependent reward misspecification. Directly explains the beta=0.1 vs 0.3 landscape.\n- Bondarenko et al. (2025) — specification gaming in reasoning models. Most directly relevant empirical precedent.\n- Kim et al. (2026, VerifAI-2) — \"Do LLMs Game Formalization?\" Studies the exact phenomenon this paper addresses, in Lean 4. Not citing this is a significant omission.\n\n**Process reward / structural reward (critical omissions):**\n- Lightman et al. (2023, ICLR 2024) — process vs. outcome supervision. ISR is conceptually a process-level signal.\n- Gao et al. (2024, arXiv 2410.15115) — showing process/outcome rewards can hurt RL training. Directly explains the paper's negative results.\n- PROF (Ye et al. 2025) — process-outcome reward harmonization. Relevant to the reward composition problem.\n\n**Neurosymbolic reasoning (missing competitors):**\n- Logic-RL (Xian et al. 2025) — rule-based RL for logical reasoning, direct competitor.\n- SatLM (Ye et al. 2023, NeurIPS) — LLM + SAT solver pipeline.\n- LINC (Olausson et al. 2023, EMNLP) — LLM + FOL prover, direct competitor.\n- Logic-LM (Pan et al. 2023, EMNLP Findings) — LLM + symbolic solver.\n- RLSF (Jha et al. 2024) — solver certificates as token-level reward. Most directly comparable reward design.\n\n**Mutation testing (conceptual ancestor, completely absent):**\n- DeMillo et al. (1978), Jia & Harman (2011 survey). ISR is a mutation score. Not citing this lineage is a significant scholarly gap.\n\n**Verdict on reference count:** 7 references is unacceptable for any ML venue. Even a short paper at ACL/EMNLP typically cites 25-40 works. The paper needs at minimum 25 references, covering the categories above.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Positioning and Framing\n\n**Differentiation from prior work.** The paper's positioning is unclear in several ways. It does not engage with Kim et al. (2026), which studies formalization gaming in Lean 4 — a paper that addresses the same phenomenon with a different solver. It does not engage with RLSF (Jha et al. 2024), which uses solver certificates as fine-grained reward — the closest methodological comparison. Without these comparisons, it is difficult to assess whether the findings are genuinely new or confirmatory.\n\n**\"Meta-level Goodhart\" framing.** This framing risks overclaiming. The phenomenon — a diagnostic metric gets gamed when used as reward — is simply Goodhart's Law applied to a new proxy. Calling it \"meta-level\" implies a recursive or higher-order structure that does not actually exist. The metric is not detecting Goodhart's Law and then being Goodharted in a self-referential way; it is simply a proxy reward being optimized, like any other. The term is catchy but potentially misleading. A more precise framing: \"ISR, originally designed as a diagnostic, fails as a reward signal due to standard Goodhart effects, with the specific gaming mode (H7) being ISR-specific.\"\n\n**Claim 3 (cross-logic).** The SMT-LIB and joint-training results in the paper draft appear to go beyond what the experimental evidence supports. The project files indicate SMT-LIB \"fails entirely\" and joint training causes \"catastrophic interference,\" yet the paper draft presents clean tables with specific numbers for SMT-LIB (PR=0.70, ISR=0.06) and joint training. The Prolog STA s43/s44 experiments were still running at the time of the draft. If any of these numbers are projected rather than measured, this is a serious concern. The reviewers should verify that all reported numbers correspond to completed experiments.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Broader Impact and Significance\n\n**For the formal reasoning community:** The finding that prove rate alone is misleading is valuable but not entirely new — Kim et al. (2026) make the same point for Lean 4. The specific diagnostic (ISR via LOO) is practical and deployable, which is a strength.\n\n**Generalizability:** The experiments are conducted on FOLIO-style problems with Qwen3-1.7B/8B. Whether the hacking taxonomy generalizes to harder problems (miniF2F, competition math), larger models (70B+), or different RL algorithms (PPO, REINFORCE++) is unknown. The paper's cautionary message about proxy metrics is general, but the specific findings are narrow.\n\n**Broader proxy-metric-as-reward caution:** This is the paper's most broadly applicable takeaway. The empirical demonstration that even a well-designed structural metric gets gamed is a useful cautionary data point for the reward design community. However, this point has been made theoretically (Skalse et al. 2022) and empirically in other domains (Gao et al. 2023). The contribution is confirmatory rather than pioneering.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Key Strengths and Weaknesses\n\n**Strengths:**\n\n1. **Rigorous controls.** The 3-seed design with random-reward controls, SFT baseline, and premise-count-matched analysis demonstrates careful experimental methodology. The random-reward control confirming H7=0% across all seeds is a clean result.\n\n2. **Honest negative results.** The paper transparently reports that ISR fails as a reward at every dose tested, and that the initial 2.4x improvement was a statistical artifact. This intellectual honesty is commendable and rare.\n\n3. **Premise-count-matched methodology.** Identifying and controlling for the component-count bias in LOO metrics is a genuine methodological contribution applicable beyond this paper.\n\n4. **Temporal ISR collapse trajectory.** The 100-step to 1000-step ISR collapse (0.64 -> 0.13 -> 0.003) while PR stays at 93-96% is a clean and striking demonstration of hidden structural degeneration.\n\n5. **Comprehensive Goodhart landscape.** Table 7 (paper framing) covering 10+ conditions with consistent evaluation is thorough and informative.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n\n1. **Severely insufficient related work.** 7 references is not viable for any ML venue. The paper fails to cite direct competitors (RLSF, Logic-RL, Kim et al. 2026), foundational work (mutation testing, Skalse et al. 2022), and relevant process reward literature. This is the most critical weakness.\n\n2. **Overclaimed novelty.** \"Meta-level Goodhart\" is standard Goodhart applied to a new proxy. LOO on solver inputs is a straightforward application of mutation testing. The paper should position itself as an empirical case study, not as introducing new concepts.\n\n3. **Questionable data integrity for Claim 3.** The paper draft reports SMT-LIB numbers, joint-training numbers, and curriculum ablation numbers that do not appear in the project's experimental record. The ROADMAP shows Prolog STA s43/s44 still running. If these are projected or hallucinated by the paper-writing agent, this undermines the paper's credibility. At minimum, Claim 3 should be restricted to completed experiments.\n\n4. **Narrow experimental scope.** Single model family (Qwen3), single RL algorithm (GRPO), single problem domain (FOLIO-style NLI), small eval set (100 problems). The 8B single-seed result is preliminary. Claims about generality require broader validation.\n\n5. **ISR's fundamental limitation acknowledged but not resolved.** ISR measures syntactic necessity, not semantic faithfulness (rho=-0.049 with NLI accuracy). This means ISR cannot distinguish between a proof that is tight-but-wrong and one that is tight-and-right. The paper acknowledges this but offers no path forward, limiting practical impact.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Data verification for Claim 3:** Can you confirm that all numbers reported in Tables 5-8 (SMT-LIB, joint training, curriculum, 8B scaling) correspond to completed experiments with full evaluation? The project records suggest some may be incomplete or projected.\n\n2. **Why not cite mutation testing?** ISR is essentially a first-order mutation score applied to proof components. The mutation testing literature (DeMillo 1978, Jia & Harman 2011) provides the theoretical foundation for why single-component deletion suffices (coupling effect). This omission is puzzling.\n\n3. **How does ISR behave on problems where genuine multi-step reasoning is required?** FOLIO problems are relatively shallow. On problems requiring 5+ step proofs, does ISR still detect hacking effectively, or does the O(N) cost become prohibitive and the signal noisier?\n\n4. **Have you considered Shapley values instead of LOO?** LOO measures marginal necessity but misses interaction effects. A premise might be individually unnecessary but necessary in combination with another. Shapley values would capture this but at exponential cost. Is there evidence that interaction effects matter for your hacking taxonomy?\n\n5. **The \"meta-level Goodhart\" framing — how is this different from standard Goodhart?** Concretely, if I replace ISR with any other bounded [0,1] proxy correlated with proof quality, would I not observe the same phenomenon? What makes ISR special in this regard?\n\n---\n\n### 7. Overall Score and Confidence\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High — familiar with reward hacking, Goodhart's Law, neurosymbolic reasoning, and mutation testing literatures)**\n\n**One-line summary:** A well-controlled empirical case study of Goodhart's Law in formal reasoning GRPO, with a useful diagnostic metric (ISR) and honest negative results, but undermined by severely insufficient related work, overclaimed novelty (\"meta-level Goodhart\" is standard Goodhart), and questionable completeness of cross-logic experiments.\n\n**Detailed justification:** The paper sits at the borderline. Its strengths — rigorous controls, transparent negative results, premise-count-matched methodology — are genuine and valuable. The ISR collapse trajectory (0.64 -> 0.003 while PR stays 93-96%) is a clean and memorable finding. However, the paper reads as if written in a bibliographic vacuum: 7 references for a topic with extensive prior work is disqualifying in its current form. The \"meta-level Goodhart\" framing overclaims what is a standard Goodhart effect on a new proxy. And the reported results for SMT-LIB, joint training, curriculum schedules, and 8B scaling appear to extend beyond completed experiments, which is a serious concern if confirmed. With a proper literature review (25+ references), honest repositioning as an empirical case study rather than a conceptual advance, and restriction to fully completed experiments, this could be a solid Findings-level paper. The premise-count-matched methodology alone is a contribution worth publishing, and the H7-as-ISR-specific-gaming finding (0% in random controls across all seeds) is clean evidence. But the current version needs substantial revision."}],"totalDurationMs":128090,"totalTokens":60867,"totalToolUseCount":9,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":700,"cache_read_input_tokens":56529,"output_tokens":3635,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":700},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3635,"cache_read_input_tokens":56529,"cache_creation_input_tokens":700,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":700,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":1,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":7}}}
